WEBVTT
TITLE Ethernet bridging and the learning switch: forwarding table, flood-forward-filter, loops and the spanning tree — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A bridge joins two segments. It reads the destination MAC address and nothing else.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
A frame from A to D crosses, because D is on the far side. A frame to B would not.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Bridge and router differ in what they read and in what they leave behind.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Collision domains: 2. Broadcast domains: 1. The bridge changed only the first.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Step one: learn the source address, with the port the frame arrived on.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Step two: look the destination up in the table as it stood before this frame.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Step three: not in the table, flood. Same port, filter. Another port, forward.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Entries age out after 300 s of silence, so a station that never sends is never learned.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Frame one: A to B. The table is empty, so the frame floods to three ports.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Frame two: B replies. A is known on port 1, so the frame goes to one port only.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Frame seven: E sends to D, and D is on the ingress port, so the bridge filters.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Eight frames: two floods, five forwards, one filter, eleven copies. Four table entries.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
One hub and four PCs: one collision domain, one broadcast domain.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
One switch and four PCs: four collision domains, still one broadcast domain.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Two switches joined: seven collision domains, one broadcast domain.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
Put a router between them: the broadcast domains become two. A hub on one port stays one.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Four bridges, fully meshed. Each has three trunks and one host port.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
One broadcast is flooded three ways: three frames. Each of those is flooded three ways again: nine.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
Ten rounds cost 4602 frames, and the count is still rising. Nothing ever dies.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
The table never settles either: the same source arrives on two ports in turn.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
The bridge ID is a 2-octet priority and a 6-octet MAC. The lowest ID wins.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
A BPDU carries the root ID, the root path cost, the sender ID, the port ID and the timers.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Root path cost is accumulated along the way: 19 to B2, 19 to B4, 38 to B3.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
A tie at equal cost is broken by the lower neighbouring bridge ID: B3 goes through B2.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Every non-root bridge picks one root port: the port on its cheapest path to the root.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Every segment picks one designated port: the end with the lower root path cost.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Every other port is blocked: B3 towards B4, and B4 towards B2. Two ports, two loops cut.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Three forwarding links from five: E - (B - 1) = 5 - 3 = 2 blocked, and the rest is a tree.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
A port that must start forwarding goes blocking, listening, learning, forwarding.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Each of those two waits is the forward delay: 15 seconds by default, so 30 seconds to forward.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Worst case after a change: max age 20 plus 15 plus 15, which is 50 seconds.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Hello is 2 s and the MAC ageing time is 300 s: different timers, different jobs.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
A full frame is 1514 octets: 14 of header and 1500 of payload, so 12112 bits.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Store and forward waits the whole 121.12 microseconds before it decides anything.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Cut-through waits for 6 octets, 0.48 microseconds. Fragment-free waits for 64, 5.12.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
The store-and-forward wait is about 252 times the cut-through wait, and it buys the CRC check.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Four steps: which device, which table state, which action, which topology.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
The invariants: one domain per switch port, one entry per source, E minus B minus one blocked ports.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Trap one: a switch splits the broadcast domain. Trap two: it learns destinations.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Say the unit first: domains, entries, copies, ports or seconds. Then count.

